1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:11,060
In 2025, Europe lived through its worst fire year on record... more than a million hectares burned. And the fires are expected to grow.

2
00:00:11,600 --> 00:00:26,560
But here's the catch. More dots on a fire map don't always mean more fire. Newer satellites see smaller fires than the old ones. Merge their counts naively, and Lebanon's fires look five times worse than they really were.

3
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:44,760
This is CEDRUS. Using NASA FIRMS data, we learn how the new VIIRS sensors translate into the old MODIS scale, from the years both flew together... and build one consistent fire record, from 2000 to today.

4
00:00:47,500 --> 00:00:51,000
Pick a region... like Lebanon, our home.

5
00:00:51,500 --> 00:01:00,800
Every month is a tile. Click October 2024, and see how it compares to a normal October, with an honest trust label.

6
00:01:02,300 --> 00:01:05,400
Switch between burning days and hotspots.

7
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:10,700
Draw any area.

8
00:01:16,900 --> 00:01:19,200
See when the fire season runs.

9
00:01:20,200 --> 00:01:23,980
And compare right now to normal, updated every day.

10
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:37,160
And to help prevent the next fire: a seven-day fire-weather outlook... heat, dryness, wind and soil moisture, compared with what's normal for that place.

11
00:01:37,800 --> 00:01:48,840
We tested it in a sealed exam, on four regions the model had never seen. Our error: 11.6 percent... against 20.3 for a simple conversion.

12
00:01:49,400 --> 00:01:58,200
For forest teams, civil protection and researchers: a clearer fire history, from changing satellites. CEDRUS.
